1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,759
Sequential streams made bulk work
declarative. Looking at a long CPU-heavy
map, a temptation appears: add .parallel()
and wait for a speedup. Parallel streams
are a power tool — and a foot-gun on IO
and tiny data.

2
00:00:13,879 --> 00:00:28,039
Suppose you transform a large list of in-
memory records with a pure, expensive
function — compress a buffer, score a
document, compute a hash. The work is
independent: one element's result does not
depend on another's.

3
00:00:28,159 --> 00:00:39,159
That is the profile where parallel streams
can help. Look at this java for a moment.
Walk the claim carefully. parallelStream()
splits the source and runs pieces
concurrently.

4
00:00:39,279 --> 00:00:51,039
Under the hood it uses the common
ForkJoinPool. Your lambdas must tolerate
running on multiple threads. Prefer purity
— no unsynchronized mutation of shared
collections inside the map.

5
00:00:51,159 --> 00:01:01,839
Best for CPU-heavy independent work on
sizable data. What goes wrong when the
problem is smaller or messier? On tiny
lists, the cost of splitting and joining
dwarfs the work.

6
00:01:01,959 --> 00:01:14,280
You can make a sort slower by
parallelizing it. Measure; don't hope.
Ordering and side effects get weird.
Encounter order may differ. forEach on a
parallel stream does not promise sequence.

7
00:01:14,399 --> 00:01:25,759
If you mutate an external ArrayList from
parallel threads, you corrupt it. Look at
this java for a moment. Use a proper
concurrent collector or keep the pipeline
pure and collect at the end.

8
00:01:25,879 --> 00:01:31,640
IO-bound work wants explicit executors or
virtual threads, not the common fork-join
pool.

9
00:01:31,759 --> 00:01:42,799
A parallel stream that blocks on network
calls inside map can stall shared compute
work for the whole JVM process — other
unrelated parallel streams may wait. Look
at this java for a moment.

10
00:01:42,919 --> 00:01:55,479
A fair check: when would you refuse
parallel streams? Tiny data, IO inside the
pipeline, need for strict encounter order
with side effects, or already saturated
cores. When would you consider them?

11
00:01:55,659 --> 00:02:10,219
Large in-memory data, pure CPU work,
associative reductions, measured
improvement. What if we always call
parallel() for fashion? Look at this java
for a moment. Three elements. No win.
Harder debugging.

12
00:02:10,339 --> 00:02:19,699
Keep sequential as the default; go
parallel when evidence says so. Reductions
have associativity requirements under
parallelism. Summing integers works.

13
00:02:19,819 --> 00:02:32,659
Prefer Collectors.joining or concurrent
collectors designed for the job. Look at
this java for a moment. Primitive
specialized streams avoid boxing and
parallelize cleanly for numeric work.

14
00:02:32,779 --> 00:02:46,579
Also know the pool: the common
ForkJoinPool size defaults around
available processors. Saturating it with
blocking tasks hurts unrelated parallel
streams library-wide. Thread-safety of the
source matters.

15
00:02:46,699 --> 00:03:01,699
Parallel streams over freshly built
ArrayLists of immutable data are the happy
path. Stabilize inputs first. Naming tip:
processInParallel(data) as a dedicated
method signals intent and localizes the
parallel boundary.

16
00:03:01,819 --> 00:03:16,819
Independent CPU work suggested
parallelism. The common ForkJoinPool
powered parallel streams. Purity and
sizing constraints appeared. Ordering and
shared mutation failed loudly. IO-bound
work pointed elsewhere.

17
00:03:16,939 --> 00:03:26,299
Measurement closed the argument. One
leftover from every pipeline — parallel or
not — still bites at the edges: a stage
that might produce no value.

18
00:03:26,419 --> 00:03:37,819
findFirst on an empty stream, a lookup
inside a map operation, a parse that
fails. Returning null from those moments
recreates the same silent absence problem
collections already taught us to hate.

19
00:03:37,939 --> 00:03:44,499
How do we make "maybe none" part of the
type at the boundary? That pressure is
what Optional exists to answer.
